  "youtube": {
    "title": "February 30: How Did Sweden Get a Day That Shouldn't Exist?",
    "description": "February 30 really happened once, in Sweden, in 1712. In 1700 Sweden began a slow switch from the Julian to the Gregorian calendar by skipping every leap day for forty years. The leap day of 1700 was skipped, but in the chaos of the Great Northern War 1704 and 1708 kept theirs, and Sweden matched no one. King Charles XII gave up and returned to the old calendar, which needed one extra day: so February 1712 had a 29th and a 30th. Sweden finally switched to the Gregorian calendar in 1753, when 17 February was followed by 1 March.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for a strange true story every day. Would you have wanted to be born on February 30th? Tell us in the comments!\n\n#shorts #february30 #stickstory",
